WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.150
- Welcome, welcome.

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.380
Thank you so much for coming.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.130
It's great to have all of you here today

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:12.963
on this beautiful day,

00:00:12.963 --> 00:00:14.630
I mean really one of the
most beautiful days we've had

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:16.350
in New Haven, weather-wise,
for a long time,

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:19.349
and here you are indoors
thanks to your dedication

00:00:19.349 --> 00:00:21.970
to the work that you're
gonna talk about today

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:23.200
and hear others describe.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.630
I'm Steve Pitti, I'm
faculty in American Studies

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:29.384
and ER&amp;M in History, and the
Director of the RITM Center,

00:00:29.384 --> 00:00:31.564
and we've been looking
forward to this event

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.540
for a long time.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.610
We were looking for an opportunity
to see you all in action,

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.350
to see people present about their work,

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.329
and just as importantly,
maybe more importantly,

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:44.050
give people the opportunity
to hear from one another,

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:46.844
because I think one of the
things that this place doesn't do

00:00:46.844 --> 00:00:51.340
as well as it might is
give folks the opportunity

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.010
to engage with one another
about their work-in-progress,

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.750
about their research in progress.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.500
Too often, all of us are sort
of dug in to our own projects,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.200
stuck in our holes,
digging, digging, digging,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.480
and we may not know who's digging what

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.600
just a few feet away from us.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.900
So here we have that opportunity, right,

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.113
to learn from one another,
to think collaboratively,

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:15.543
to think across disciplines,
all more or less around topics

00:01:15.543 --> 00:01:19.310
related to questions
surrounding race, indigeneity,

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:22.390
transnational migration,
and similar things.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.394
So last year here in the RITM Center,

00:01:24.394 --> 00:01:27.334
we had the opportunity, the
extraordinary opportunity

00:01:27.334 --> 00:01:31.920
to support research
work by undergraduates,

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.230
graduate students, and professional
students around campus,

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:39.184
a total of 52 students from
all categories received

00:01:39.184 --> 00:01:41.930
some form of support from us,

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.530
and we're really, really
fortunate and proud

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:47.470
that we were able to support
students from so many different

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:50.830
parts of the university, from
17 different departments,

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:54.207
programs, and divisions of the
university, both in this part

00:01:54.207 --> 00:01:56.663
of campus, Central Campus, the
faculty of Arts &amp; Sciences,

00:01:56.663 --> 00:01:59.386
Yale College, the Yale Graduate
School, but also extending

00:01:59.386 --> 00:02:02.270
out to the medical school,
the nursing school,

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.793
the art school, architecture, divinity,

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.536
forestry and environmental
studies, and so forth.

00:02:08.536 --> 00:02:12.820
So this is what part of
our mandate is here in RITM

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:17.160
is to try to nurture and
enable that work in all parts

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.920
where we can help, and then
to bring that work together

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.310
in one place as best we can to make sure

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:25.890
that you all can hear from one another.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.450
So we're excited about the
opportunity today to bring

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.327
that work together, to give
you the chance to present

00:02:31.327 --> 00:02:35.320
and to hear from one another
about the work that you've done

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.060
and to make this space available,
to enable collaboration

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:44.410
and elaboration in the hour or so to come.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.349
So what we have today is a
subset, a fraction of the work

00:02:48.349 --> 00:02:53.349
that happened over the summer,

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.920
I think 12 presentations.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.573
And we thank all of you
for being here today,

00:02:58.573 --> 00:03:02.890
for answering the call as summer awardees

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:05.470
to come and share your
work with one another.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:06.720
Thanks so much.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.490
What we're gonna ask
everybody to do is to speak

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:11.421
for no more than three
minutes about their work.

00:03:11.421 --> 00:03:14.090
We have put together a program,

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:19.090
and the PowerPoint presentations
are already in order,

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.280
so they will load automatically,

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.164
and that should make this
easier for everybody.

00:03:25.164 --> 00:03:28.050
As you can see I'm holding a microphone.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.168
We ask you to also hold the
microphone so we can catch this

00:03:30.168 --> 00:03:35.168
on tape, and make sure
that this is recorded well.

00:03:36.589 --> 00:03:38.910
We'll let you know when
you have a minute left,

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.070
and then when your time is up, so we hope

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:46.070
that you'll enjoy this,
settle in, have fun with this,

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.183
but also stick to time.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:58.970
- Hi, my name is Ambre
Dromgoole, I'm in the Departments

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:01.470
of Religious Studies and
African American Studies.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.570
Oh there we go, okay.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.410
This summer while presenting
my research on 20th century

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.310
Gospel composer Roxie Ann
Moore at the Biannual Christian

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.860
Congregational Music
Conference in Oxford, England,

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.220
I had the opportunity to
converse and fellowship

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.540
with a group of scholars
researching Black sacred music

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.660
traditions spanning the Atlantic.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.000
These discussions opened the
door for sustained global

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.300
conversation and collaboration
concerning the nature

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:28.370
of Black sound, consciousness,
and self-making.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:31.070
From United States 20th century
Great Migration narratives

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.280
and the lasting impact of
Black Southern migrants

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.910
to Northern cities, on
Black expressive culture,

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:38.640
to the onset and effect
of the mid-20th century

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.870
Windrush movement of Caribbean
and West African populations,

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:44.290
creating a multi-faceted
Black-British community,

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:46.050
negotiating collective significance

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.400
against nuanced cultural
offerings and traditions,

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.880
the dialogue was incredibly dynamic.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.050
This to say, in addition
to my own research

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.380
concerning the creation,
evolution, and innovation

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:58.420
of Black musical production
and sound as analyzed

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:00.900
and described through the
life of Roxie Ann Moore,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.120
I was able to add this
Transatlantic component

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.930
to which she was also a
contributor and beneficiary

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.110
to my research which was a direct result

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.023
of my travels and RITM support.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.540
While my initial work this
summer was supposed to follow

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:16.580
the trails of Roxie Ann Moore
and legendary 20th century

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:18.738
vocal group, the Golden Gate
Quartet, I found my work

00:05:18.738 --> 00:05:21.732
taking different turns as a
result of robust conversation

00:05:21.732 --> 00:05:24.410
held with my Black, British colleagues.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:27.610
For example, letters I
found in Roxie's archive

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:29.937
prior to my travels
informed me of how the Gates

00:05:29.937 --> 00:05:33.210
first arrived in Paris in
the mid-1950s to explore

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.310
new economic opportunities
after their light began to dim

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:38.840
in the United States, and music
labels started casting them

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.250
aside for seemingly brighter stars.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:43.470
I knew that Black Americans
in the mid-20th century

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:45.666
viewed Paris as a place
of freedom and liberation

00:05:45.666 --> 00:05:48.850
from the economic and racial
turmoil of the United States,

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:51.560
and that the ambassadorship of
jazz artists opened a window

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.600
for other Black musical artists and groups

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:56.990
like the Golden Gate Quartet
to find audiences in Europe.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:05:59.590
But through this summer's
research, I learned that Windrush,

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:02.410
the mass migration of West
Indian and West African migrants

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.343
to Europe from the mid
to late 20th century

00:06:04.343 --> 00:06:06.734
coincided with these
efforts, adding several other

00:06:06.734 --> 00:06:10.223
dimensions to an ever-evolving
Black European soundscape.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:13.160
During the Christian
Congregational Music Conference,

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:15.540
I learned that Trinbagonian
folk songs that migrants

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:17.672
brought with them, songs
combined with the sounds

00:06:17.672 --> 00:06:20.710
of a burgeoning Gospel music industry,

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:22.790
scholars observed how
the commercialization

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:25.385
and commodification of Black
American sacred music practices

00:06:25.385 --> 00:06:28.906
affected Caribbean and West
African artistic retentions

00:06:28.906 --> 00:06:31.440
as they sought to adapt to new landscapes

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.870
while keeping home near.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:36.310
Discussion surrounded the
Americanization of dialect

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.360
and pronunciation when
recording music came up

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:39.870
time and time again.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:41.950
This experience has shifted
my research in a new

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:44.490
and dynamic directions,
and I'm grateful to RITM

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:46.340
for allowing me the opportunity to open

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.933
such a necessary dialogue, thank you.

00:06:48.933 --> 00:06:51.183
(applause)

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:00.340
- Hi, I'm Michelle Johnson.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.538
I am a second-year in History
and African American studies.

00:07:03.538 --> 00:07:06.270
So my research looked at
the Civil Rights movement,

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.108
generally it looks at civil
rights movements in places

00:07:08.108 --> 00:07:09.690
outside of the South.

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.880
So I start with the Great
Migration, starting around 1916

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:14.940
when Black folks were
moving from the South

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.360
up to Northern industrial
cities, and from there

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.014
I look at how those same
Black folks in cities

00:07:20.014 --> 00:07:21.980
took their Southern roots,
took what was happening

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:24.920
in the South, and made the
Civil Rights movement their own.

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:28.135
So as Black Southerners moved
into Chicago specifically,

00:07:28.135 --> 00:07:31.540
they set about rebuilding and
creating new institutions.

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:34.250
These included churches and
schools, as well as branches

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:36.218
of organizations like the NAACP

00:07:36.218 --> 00:07:38.680
and Congress of Racial Equality.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:40.810
Early on in the Civil Rights
era, many of these groups

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:42.770
were strategically made interracial,

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:45.800
which is how I found myself,
kind of unexpectedly,

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.856
looking at the papers of
Faith Rich, a white woman

00:07:47.856 --> 00:07:50.890
who worked as an unpaid
staffer for the Chicago NAACP

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:53.260
beginning in 1945 and working all the way

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:55.130
until she passed away in the 1990s.

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:58.240
In her more than 50 years
living in a Black neighborhood

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.305
on the West Side of Chicago,
she involved herself

00:08:00.305 --> 00:08:03.630
in nearly every education-related
battle for equality,

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:07.780
as well as political struggles
over urban renewal policies.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:11.330
Though much of her work was
working on creating research

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:13.500
for reports, she was also not afraid

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:16.040
of door-to-door organizing,
doing a lot of block work,

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:18.800
or block-based organizing work.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:20.520
Chicago's Civil Rights
movements were in some ways

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:22.330
very similar to movements in the South.

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:24.500
Much of the organizing
started with desegregating

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:27.005
restaurants and roller rinks,
but then went on to schools

00:08:27.005 --> 00:08:29.730
and eventually housing,
and then eventually a lot

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:33.093
of Black Power-ish movements
working on Black business.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:38.030
These individual movements
sort of broken down

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:40.443
issue by issue were also
happening all at once

00:08:40.443 --> 00:08:45.443
throughout time and ended up
being continued over the period

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.670
starting around 1916 to 1970,
so while you can break them

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:51.570
down issue by issue, they also
were concurrent throughout.

00:08:52.618 --> 00:08:55.388
In one of the most
dramatic moments in 1963,

00:08:55.388 --> 00:08:58.750
200,000 public school students
stayed home from school

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:01.120
to protest inequality in public education.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:04.920
Three years later, before the
schools came to a resolution,

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.060
Martin Luther King moved
into the city to fight

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:08.270
for open housing.

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:11.160
Local people on the ground
juggled these shifting priorities

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:13.270
as focus changed over time.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:15.770
The majority of my sources up
to this point, doing research

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:18.590
for a couple years, were
not personal in nature.

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:19.855
So I looked a lot of reports created

00:09:19.855 --> 00:09:22.040
by community organizations,
internal memos,

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:24.340
drafts of speeches, things like that,

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:26.410
but this was one of the first
archives that I came across

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:27.700
that had letters back and forth

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:29.530
to husband and wife, for example.

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:31.280
And I had to figure out
what to make of that.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:32.580
It's a different type of source.

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:35.090
I had never had to come
across it, what do you do?

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:37.046
And I found that actually
it was really helpful

00:09:37.046 --> 00:09:39.733
in piecing together what
the role of a white woman

00:09:39.733 --> 00:09:44.370
in a Black activist position meant

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:46.140
and how she felt about
it and how she took it on

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:47.260
and how her neighbors felt about it

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:48.840
and how her family felt about it.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.080
So it really shifted
my focus on my research

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:54.130
into not only Black folks
organizing about issues

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:57.100
that affected them, but
also the ways that coalition

00:09:57.100 --> 00:10:00.260
was working in the city
of Chicago in this period,

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:01.443
thank you.

00:10:01.443 --> 00:10:03.693
(applause)

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:14.780
- Hi, my name is Heidi,
I'm a senior History major

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:15.780
here at Yale.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:17.710
This summer I was fortunate
to receive funding

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:20.153
from the RITM Center to
visit a series of archives

00:10:20.153 --> 00:10:21.628
in the Midwest.

00:10:21.628 --> 00:10:24.750
In particular, I was investigating
the relationship between,

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:27.680
the entangled relationship
between railroad expansion

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:29.350
and the White Earth and
Red Lake reservations

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:30.947
in Northwestern Minnesota in the late 19th

00:10:30.947 --> 00:10:32.573
and early 20th centuries.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.460
What I found increasingly
in my research by looking

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:39.600
at Indian Nation correspondence
and diaries and maps

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:41.970
and transcribed oral histories

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:46.120
was how much railroads really
affected both reservation

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:51.120
policies and Euro-American
settlers' access to these lands.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.238
So in 1889, the Nelson Act
was established in Minnesota

00:10:56.238 --> 00:10:59.890
that basically granted
settlers, Euro-American settlers

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:03.410
access and settlement
upon both the White Earth

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:06.130
and Red Lake reservations and
also allowed timber tycoons

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:07.900
to access these reservations.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:11.150
So I started looking at
maps, for example from 1887

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:13.560
that showed a transcontinental
railroad passing

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:16.107
just south of the reservation
and all the lands in orange

00:11:16.107 --> 00:11:19.630
represent lands that
were available for sale.

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:22.520
This reveals the degree of
pressure that railroads applied

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:25.740
on these reservations and
therefore allowed for settlement

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:27.350
on the reservations.

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:29.730
Likewise, only 15 years
later, we see the effect

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:31.860
of regional railroads and
the presence of railroads

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:33.320
on the reservations.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:35.770
So this railroad, the Sioux
line, passed right through

00:11:35.770 --> 00:11:38.340
the heart of White Earth
and just skirted Red Lake.

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:41.010
That allowed timber tycoons
and land speculators

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:44.040
to gain access to the
land, which in turn allowed

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:47.530
for the denuding of the
landscape and white settlers

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:50.440
to basically take over these reservations.

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:54.457
The effects of this railroad expansion

00:11:54.457 --> 00:11:57.010
was just devastating for
the Native populations.

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:59.305
Here we see an Ojibwe woman practicing

00:11:59.305 --> 00:12:02.850
her traditional maple
sugaring, and likewise,

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:05.970
other practices such as wild
ricing became very endangered,

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.160
because of the intensive logging practices

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.320
that were occurring because
of the railroad expansion.

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:15.380
And so we see basically
that how greatly railroads

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:19.610
are implicated in this process
of environmental degradation,

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:22.337
land dispossession, and
the cultural practices

00:12:22.337 --> 00:12:25.850
that suffered thereafter.

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:28.370
And so I was just super
fortunate to engage with a series

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:31.580
of photographs such as the
one with Roland Reed here,

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:34.390
and all these different
maps, and Indian Nation

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:38.710
correspondence that just
shed light on this story

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:40.660
that's otherwise been somewhat neglected

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:43.170
in the historiography of the period.

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:46.870
Most people have focused on
just the policies themselves

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:48.830
instead of the vehicles
that allowed these policies

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:51.210
to be enacted, so thank you.

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:53.460
(applause)

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:07.610
- Hi, my name is Jacqueline
Ly, I'm a third-year History

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:10.620
Ph.D student studying
Latin American history.

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:13.380
And this summer I went
to London for my research

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:15.490
thanks to money from RITM.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:17.280
So my research is on the Bay of Honduras

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.772
and the Mosquito shore
in the 18th century.

00:13:19.772 --> 00:13:22.050
So the Bay of Honduras occupied the region

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:24.630
that is today Belize, you'll
see it in yellow on the map

00:13:24.630 --> 00:13:26.170
labeled British Honduras.

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:28.400
And the Mosquito Shore
occupied the Caribbean coast

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:31.100
of modern-day Southern
Honduras and Nicaragua,

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:33.230
so it comes down, the pink and green here

00:13:33.230 --> 00:13:35.239
on the right side of the screen.

00:13:35.239 --> 00:13:38.420
So the significance of the
time and place come into play

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:41.160
in the context of early
modern Atlantic empires.

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:44.340
Here specifically we're
dealing with Britain and Spain,

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:46.720
and Spain by right of
discovery and the authority

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:49.932
of the Papal Bull of 1493
claimed absolute sovereignty

00:13:49.932 --> 00:13:53.370
over the entire expanse of
the Americas west of the line

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:56.130
drawn in the Treaty of Tordesillas.

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:58.840
So of course, this is
how it worked in theory,

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:00.815
but in reality, Spain
didn't have the manpower

00:14:00.815 --> 00:14:03.100
and the Spanish authorities on the ground

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:05.480
didn't have the inclination
to completely explore

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:08.650
and take a hold of the territory
in all of Spanish America.

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:10.956
And especially on this
Caribbean coast of New Spain,

00:14:10.956 --> 00:14:13.330
the terrain was jungle-filled
and the coastline

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:15.564
was treacherous, and so
Europeans really made few entries

00:14:15.564 --> 00:14:17.520
into this region.

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:19.687
The British eventually
came to get a foothold here

00:14:19.687 --> 00:14:23.624
in the Bay in particular,
in the yellow area.

00:14:23.624 --> 00:14:26.220
And as the historiographic myth goes,

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:29.460
in the Golden Age of Buccaneering
as it comes to an end,

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:32.080
the buccaneers who would've
been among the best sailors

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:35.089
of this region would have
been able to navigate

00:14:35.089 --> 00:14:37.800
the treacherous Keys and
reefs coming into the Bay,

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:40.420
so you can see the map of the water

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:41.720
as it moves around these islands.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:43.740
It would've been really
hard to actually access

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:47.005
the landmass of this
coast of Central America.

00:14:47.005 --> 00:14:50.531
So as Jamaica changes
hands from Spain to Britain

00:14:50.531 --> 00:14:53.900
in the late 17th century, and
sugar planters are beginning

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:56.513
to establish their
lucrative businesses here,

00:14:56.513 --> 00:14:58.623
the European powers agree
that buccaneering needed

00:14:58.623 --> 00:15:02.590
to come to an end in this new
age of order in the Caribbean.

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:04.950
So as a result, the buccaneers
who had been operating

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:07.530
in the Caribbean sought
new sources of income,

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:09.860
and they turned to enterprises
they had already been

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:12.592
starting, which were
cutting logwood and mahogany

00:15:12.592 --> 00:15:14.340
from this region.

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:18.610
So as these trades expand,
Spain and Britain begin to vie

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:21.068
for sovereignty over the region,
and the traditional story

00:15:21.068 --> 00:15:24.580
that's told of this is about
an Anglo-Spanish conflict.

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:28.220
So my question in researching
this has been challenging

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:30.650
the historiography to look at other sides

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:33.530
that are not simply about
European sovereignty,

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:35.540
and specifically about
the Mosquito Indians

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:37.803
who actually held territorial, political,

00:15:37.803 --> 00:15:40.950
and political control of this region.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:43.680
And a geographer Karl Offen
writes that this region

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.488
was only called Mosquitia
or the Mosquito Shore

00:15:45.488 --> 00:15:48.750
because of the Mosquito's
spatial practices.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:51.445
Otherwise it would have
only been some other place

00:15:51.445 --> 00:15:53.180
in Central America.

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:56.040
So my research looks at
how the Mosquito Indians

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:58.720
were not only crucial in
facilitating European forays

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:01.052
into the region, but also
I'd like to take seriously

00:16:01.052 --> 00:16:04.251
the words themselves, so for
example, why has sovereignty

00:16:04.251 --> 00:16:07.290
not been used in the
historiography of this region?

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:09.824
And how can we apply
that, taking into account

00:16:09.824 --> 00:16:13.190
contemporary challenges that
the Mosquito people still face

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:16.477
with the Nicaraguan government
and questioning the ethics

00:16:16.477 --> 00:16:19.610
and how far we can actually
push the history and questions

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:21.560
in this region, so that's my work.

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:22.867
Thank you.

00:16:22.867 --> 00:16:25.117
(applause)

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:33.670
- Hello, my name is Ever Osorio.

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:35.430
I'm a third-year in American Studies

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:37.610
and Women, Gender, and Sexuality Studies,

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:41.547
and this summer I had opportunity
to do archival research

00:16:41.547 --> 00:16:44.710
which is something that
I've never done before,

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:47.810
so I wanna thank Steve
for gently pushing me

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:50.190
in the last two years
to go beyond my methods

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:51.700
and improve them.

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:56.700
So I guess that one of the
gains was to not be scared

00:16:57.310 --> 00:17:00.740
anymore of the archive, and I
think that's a big, big step.

00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:03.180
So I had opportunity
to gather, make friends

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:06.190
with the archival people,
and get to know materials,

00:17:06.190 --> 00:17:08.826
how to do research, how
is the archive organized,

00:17:08.826 --> 00:17:11.010
so I kind of learned that.

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:14.810
And my question was, well,
what is the global circulation

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:17.530
of contemporary art on the War on Drugs?

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:20.140
And why level of circulation,
because I wanted to see

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:22.000
how there were different interpretations

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.140
of the Mexican War on
Drugs, which is my main area

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:27.758
of research, in aesthetics,
as how we are feeling

00:17:27.758 --> 00:17:30.600
this violence from a global perspective.

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:32.110
So I went to four archives.

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:33.470
The first one was MUAC,

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:36.420
which is Museo Universitario
Arte Contemporáneo,

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:38.910
which is University Museum of UNAM,

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:42.630
which is the main public
national university in Mexico.

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:44.480
And there what I found was fascinating

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:47.312
because I found embroiderers
which were women

00:17:47.312 --> 00:17:52.312
that embroidered the name of
victims of the war on drugs,

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:55.780
of casualties, so it was
very interesting to see

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:58.280
how I had forgotten about that,

00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:00.790
like I had forgotten
about all those visual

00:18:00.790 --> 00:18:03.150
artistic manifestations
that had been taking place

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:04.670
in the last 10 years,

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:06.890
so that was, in a way this research

00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:11.160
was also self-autographic,
because I found a lot of stuff

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:13.983
that I forgotten, like what
was forgotten was for sure one

00:18:13.983 --> 00:18:18.960
of the most important elements
of this historical record.

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:21.890
Then I went to Paris
to the Centre Pompidou.

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:25.730
I found nothing, but
that absence is important

00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:27.880
because it really speaks
of the circulation of art

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:31.080
and the geographical distribution

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:33.770
of certain topics or concerns.

00:18:33.770 --> 00:18:36.570
Then I went to Kassel in
Germany, where documenta,

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:39.470
one of the most important
contemporary art fairs takes place

00:18:39.470 --> 00:18:43.140
every five years, and there
what I found were more ...

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:44.810
Since I'm not looking for history

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:47.960
or a particular historiography,
I am mostly looking

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:52.800
for metaphors or, I don't like
the word "sign," but symbols

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:56.740
that let me think beyond
what I'm already thinking,

00:18:56.740 --> 00:18:59.100
so I found a lot of metaphors there.

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:02.370
And finally I was in
Venice, where the Biennale,

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:03.203
the Venice Biennale,

00:19:03.203 --> 00:19:06.550
which is the oldest contemporary
art fair, takes place.

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:07.987
And this is the work Teresa Margolles,

00:19:07.987 --> 00:19:11.606
she is one of the most relevant
contemporary Mexican artists

00:19:11.606 --> 00:19:15.120
and the name of this art
piece is "The Surge,"

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:20.120
and it's relevant because it
shows posters of the government

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:23.732
looking or searching for
women that are missing,

00:19:23.732 --> 00:19:28.732
and what is so sadly and painful

00:19:29.092 --> 00:19:33.490
is that these are from 2009, 2011,

00:19:33.490 --> 00:19:37.770
so when we see the massive
demonstrations of women

00:19:38.761 --> 00:19:42.890
in our hemisphere, we can see
how old that phenomenon is,

00:19:42.890 --> 00:19:44.740
so that's what I found.

00:19:44.740 --> 00:19:46.990
(applause)

00:19:57.350 --> 00:19:59.300
- Hello, my name is Héctor Peralta,

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:02.193
and I'm a third-year Ph.D.
student in American Studies.

00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:06.941
Today, our public schools in
the U.S. are as segregated

00:20:06.941 --> 00:20:10.040
as they were when the Supreme Court ruled

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:12.560
in Brown Vs. Board of Education.

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:14.857
What's more, states like
New York and California

00:20:14.857 --> 00:20:18.367
are among the most
segregated of those states.

00:20:18.367 --> 00:20:20.930
But how did we get here?

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:24.800
Well, by the 1980s,
Constitutionally-mandated integration

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:27.610
of schools had been largely
undermined through legislation

00:20:27.610 --> 00:20:29.620
like California's Prop 21,

00:20:29.620 --> 00:20:31.423
which prohibited mandatory busing.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:34.807
Today, schools continue to
contend with the effects

00:20:34.807 --> 00:20:38.140
of white flight, redistricting,
and the rise in private

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:41.060
and charter school enrollment,

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:43.575
yet state-funded school
programs working to improve

00:20:43.575 --> 00:20:47.820
graduation rates and increase
access to higher eduction

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:50.590
largely ignore the realities of inequity

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:52.463
produced through school segregation.

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:56.230
One such program is AVID, which stands

00:20:56.230 --> 00:20:58.990
for Advancement via
Individual Determination.

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:01.720
In 1980, a high school English teacher

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:05.100
named Mary Catherine Swanson
founded the AVID program

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:08.220
at a public high school
in San Diego County.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:10.930
As schools restructured
to accommodate for changes

00:21:10.930 --> 00:21:13.025
in student demographics
and budget capacity

00:21:13.025 --> 00:21:17.010
caused by factors like
refugee resettlement

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:20.680
and the privatization of
education, AVID was advertised

00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:22.918
as a remedy, an educational innovation

00:21:22.918 --> 00:21:25.083
to close the achievement gap.

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:29.685
Today, AVID is offered in 47 states

00:21:29.685 --> 00:21:32.900
and 16 countries worldwide.

00:21:32.900 --> 00:21:35.482
In popular coverage, Swanson
is celebrated as a visionary

00:21:35.482 --> 00:21:39.690
educator with a plan and
the gall to disseminate it.

00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:42.230
Yet these very accounts
celebrate her individual grit

00:21:42.230 --> 00:21:45.310
and tenacity without emphasizing
the collective of mentors,

00:21:45.310 --> 00:21:47.430
educators, and
administrators that make AVID

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:49.520
as successful as it is.

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:52.332
Even the name, Advancement
via Individual Determination

00:21:52.332 --> 00:21:56.390
pushes forward a neo-liberal
vision of public education.

00:21:56.390 --> 00:21:58.930
It is up to the individual
student determination

00:21:58.930 --> 00:22:01.090
if they go to college.

00:22:01.090 --> 00:22:04.104
So my research asks, how is
the resegregation of schools

00:22:04.104 --> 00:22:06.720
and the unequal distribution of resources

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:09.560
rendered invisible behind
programs like AVID?

00:22:09.560 --> 00:22:12.210
Furthermore, how is the
AVID pedagogy implemented

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:14.880
in other education programs
designed for indigenous

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:17.480
and migrant communities
within the San Diego region?

00:22:18.350 --> 00:22:20.690
With the support of RITM,
I conducted archival

00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:23.300
and ethnographic research
this summer to examine

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:25.787
how different educational
institutions in San Diego

00:22:25.787 --> 00:22:28.810
address the achievement gap in schools.

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:31.820
Primarily I worked at the
Viejas Tribal Education Center,

00:22:31.820 --> 00:22:35.030
or VTEC, located on the
Viejas-Kumeyaay Reservation

00:22:35.030 --> 00:22:37.040
in East County, San Diego.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:39.174
VTEC provides after-school
tutoring, college readiness

00:22:39.174 --> 00:22:43.010
and life management skills
workshops for students

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:46.320
who are children of
enrolled tribal members.

00:22:47.155 --> 00:22:49.690
In an oral history I
conducted with Teresa,

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:51.950
the high school student
coordinator at VTEC,

00:22:51.950 --> 00:22:53.910
she stated the greatest need
within the public school

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:56.246
district is tutoring
services for all students,

00:22:56.246 --> 00:23:00.590
not just those in AVID
or those at part of VTEC.

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:02.720
Looking forward, I will
analyze how a collaborative

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:05.780
mentorship functions
differently across AVID, VTEC,

00:23:05.780 --> 00:23:08.552
and YALLA, which is a non-profit
that uses a combination

00:23:08.552 --> 00:23:10.990
of after-school tutoring and team soccer

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:13.670
to prepare migrant
students to enter college.

00:23:13.670 --> 00:23:15.320
As San Diego County continues to lead

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:18.330
in refugee resettlement, I
hope my research will support

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:22.084
and promote pedagogies of and
for liberation, thank you.

00:23:22.084 --> 00:23:24.334
(applause)

00:23:32.870 --> 00:23:35.070
- Hi everyone, my name is Teanu Reid.

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:37.540
I am a joint-Ph.D. student in History

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:39.620
and African American Studies.

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:42.180
If I asked everyone to take
out some money right now,

00:23:42.180 --> 00:23:44.512
you might pull out a dollar bill, a coin,

00:23:44.512 --> 00:23:46.789
or even a debit or credit card.

00:23:46.789 --> 00:23:49.060
It wouldn't be difficult
for many of us in the room

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:51.750
right now, but there was a
time in the early modern period

00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:53.760
where money was not
standard, and if I asked

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:56.041
the same question in the
17th or 18th century,

00:23:56.041 --> 00:23:59.103
you might pull out a Spanish
piece of eight, a French livre,

00:23:59.103 --> 00:24:02.280
English or Dutch bills of
exchange, and you could all be

00:24:02.280 --> 00:24:04.766
in the same British colony.

00:24:04.766 --> 00:24:09.766
For my research, I am connecting
this early modern history

00:24:09.817 --> 00:24:12.490
of the complicated monetary supply

00:24:12.490 --> 00:24:14.550
with the rented out
labor and Sunday markets

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:16.610
of Africans and their descendants,

00:24:16.610 --> 00:24:19.324
particularly I am exploring
the economic activities

00:24:19.324 --> 00:24:21.940
of freed and enslaved
people of color in Barbados,

00:24:21.940 --> 00:24:26.050
Jamaica, and South
Carolina from 1650 to 1770.

00:24:26.050 --> 00:24:28.930
I am tracing their sources of income,

00:24:28.930 --> 00:24:30.570
the economies they participated in,

00:24:30.570 --> 00:24:32.690
and how their lives too were affected

00:24:32.690 --> 00:24:35.250
by the early modern monetary supply,

00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:38.690
wondering how they paid
for their manumission,

00:24:38.690 --> 00:24:42.170
wondering how they paid for
rent, for clothes, for food.

00:24:42.170 --> 00:24:44.880
Through my analysis of the
money of freed and enslaved

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:47.670
people of color, I hope to
provide an early modern basis

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:49.770
for thinking about economic inequality

00:24:49.770 --> 00:24:52.220
not only in terms of the
difference for how much money

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:54.354
people have, but also in the difference

00:24:54.354 --> 00:24:57.270
in the types of money that
people have access to.

00:24:57.270 --> 00:25:00.050
And finally I hope to create
a interdisciplinary dialogue

00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:03.480
between the fields of history,
finance, and Black studies.

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:06.220
To complete my project,
this summer with the support

00:25:06.220 --> 00:25:08.750
of RITM, I went to the
British National Archives

00:25:08.750 --> 00:25:11.930
where I explored several
colonial office records,

00:25:11.930 --> 00:25:15.070
in particular I'll name two:
the Colonial Office Records

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:19.670
2815, which contain several
letters about the illicit trade

00:25:19.670 --> 00:25:21.870
between Barbados and the French,

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:25.510
and then Colonial Office Record 137/2

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:27.570
that contains similar
documents with relation

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:31.230
to Jamaica's illicit
trade with the Spanish.

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:36.230
Collectively, what these
documents show is that,

00:25:36.447 --> 00:25:37.808
do-do-do-do-do-do-do,

00:25:37.808 --> 00:25:42.200
whether or not, sorry, one second,

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:44.780
primarily I would argue from these sources

00:25:44.780 --> 00:25:49.760
that the support for piracy
that went on sometimes

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:53.040
under the table by these
colonial governors shows

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:56.040
that there was a desperation
for money in the colonies,

00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:58.940
and that there was a definite
means to procure money

00:25:58.940 --> 00:26:01.210
through illicit trade,
and with further research,

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:04.150
I am going to show that the
currency from this trade

00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:05.960
ended up in the hands of
freed and enslaved people

00:26:05.960 --> 00:26:07.634
and was important to their survival

00:26:07.634 --> 00:26:09.689
in these colonial spaces.

00:26:09.689 --> 00:26:10.888
Thank you.

00:26:10.888 --> 00:26:13.138
(applause)

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:25.010
- Hi, my name is Gabi Rivera,
I'm Mapuche, I'm a senior,

00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:29.420
an ERM major, I'm an
undergrad, and I'm using images

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:31.918
of street art in Chile,
around Santiago and Temuco

00:26:31.918 --> 00:26:34.060
that I collected this past summer

00:26:35.050 --> 00:26:37.610
to look at indigenous
Mapuche urban identities

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:40.207
and resistance today against
state-sanctioned violences

00:26:40.207 --> 00:26:42.723
directed against indigenous
communities in Chile.

00:26:43.601 --> 00:26:46.400
And one question that
I ask is to what extent

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:48.310
are these artworks
examples of manifestations

00:26:48.310 --> 00:26:51.059
of Mapuche resistance, because
urban spaces are oftentimes

00:26:51.059 --> 00:26:54.360
not seen as spaces inhabited
by indigenous peoples,

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:57.980
and therefore this becomes
indigenous space reclamation.

00:26:57.980 --> 00:26:59.668
They can be self-erected
memorials to victims

00:26:59.668 --> 00:27:02.910
of police brutality, like Matías Catrileo,

00:27:02.910 --> 00:27:05.070
who was murdered by a police officer.

00:27:05.070 --> 00:27:07.100
They can address hydro-electric
company encroachment

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:09.941
on Mapuche lands, the
desecration of burial sites,

00:27:09.941 --> 00:27:12.050
or current Mapuche political prisoners,

00:27:12.050 --> 00:27:14.212
like Francisca Linconao who was arrested

00:27:14.212 --> 00:27:17.860
under Chile's anti-terrorism
law, because the state

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:21.060
oftentimes perceives Mapuche
people as terrorists.

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:24.240
Linconao is currently still
in prison for her resistance.

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:26.340
I also asked to what extent
do some of these murals

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:27.850
in the heart of Santiago point to an issue

00:27:27.850 --> 00:27:30.620
of multiculturalism in the Chilean state?

00:27:30.620 --> 00:27:33.230
Or the concept of promoting
diversity politics in a country

00:27:33.230 --> 00:27:35.559
oftentimes while still maintaining
discriminatory policies

00:27:35.559 --> 00:27:38.390
toward specific racial and ethnic groups.

00:27:38.390 --> 00:27:39.960
In Chile, one of the tenets that allows

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:41.764
the multicultural state
to function is the concept

00:27:41.764 --> 00:27:45.547
of "mestizaje," which is
typically defined as the mixing

00:27:45.547 --> 00:27:49.460
of white-Spanish, or colonizer
blood, with indigenous blood,

00:27:49.460 --> 00:27:52.450
to produce a quote-unquote
new race, or the Mestizo.

00:27:52.450 --> 00:27:54.306
And I'm going to refer
to it as an ideology

00:27:54.306 --> 00:27:59.306
in an engagement with Mapuche
poet Daniela Catrileo's work.

00:27:59.765 --> 00:28:03.280
She refers to mestizaje
as a colonizer ideology

00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:05.430
which promotes the assimilation
of indigenous peoples

00:28:05.430 --> 00:28:07.210
into white society.

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:08.935
And this further supports
the settler state

00:28:08.935 --> 00:28:11.990
by promoting a diminishing
of indigenous communities

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:14.840
through a pattern of
self-identification with whiteness,

00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:16.370
and it creates a racial hierarchy

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:18.260
with the quote-unquote
actually indigenous peoples

00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:21.837
at the bottom and then Mestizos
and whiteness on the top.

00:28:21.837 --> 00:28:25.160
Many Mestizos claim Native
heritage or ancestry,

00:28:25.160 --> 00:28:27.280
which relegates our
indigeneity to the past

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:29.970
as belonging to history,
rather than as an active part

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:31.956
of ourselves today and
promotes stereotypes

00:28:31.956 --> 00:28:33.890
such as vanishing Native people

00:28:33.890 --> 00:28:36.140
and that indigenous
peoples are disappearing.

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:39.200
Mestizos in Chilean society
are oftentimes the one

00:28:39.200 --> 00:28:41.490
enacting violence against Mapuche people,

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:43.340
and enacting the violence
on indigenous communities

00:28:43.340 --> 00:28:46.180
that they perceive themselves
to no longer be a part of.

00:28:46.180 --> 00:28:48.330
The street art in Chile
gets at complex layerings

00:28:48.330 --> 00:28:50.710
of identities and of the presence
of a white settler state,

00:28:50.710 --> 00:28:52.360
and ultimately I'm gonna draw parallels

00:28:52.360 --> 00:28:55.359
between Chilean street art
and settler-state violences

00:28:55.359 --> 00:28:59.390
to transnational settler-state
violences protested

00:28:59.390 --> 00:29:01.930
by indigenous produced murals
in neighborhoods of color

00:29:01.930 --> 00:29:05.304
that I've also collected in
Baltimore and Washington, D.C.,

00:29:05.304 --> 00:29:07.830
like murals containing
images of Freddie Gray

00:29:07.830 --> 00:29:10.866
or Trayvon Martin, and I wanna
draw attention to a variety

00:29:10.866 --> 00:29:13.460
of human rights violations and injustices

00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:15.919
that settler-states legally
and systemically perpetuate

00:29:15.919 --> 00:29:18.259
that we continue to confront today.

00:29:18.259 --> 00:29:20.310
(speaks in foreign language)

00:29:20.310 --> 00:29:21.917
Thank you.

00:29:21.917 --> 00:29:24.167
(applause)

00:29:32.620 --> 00:29:33.930
- Hi, I'm Sylvia Ryerson.

00:29:33.930 --> 00:29:36.054
I'm a second-year American Studies Ph.D.,

00:29:36.054 --> 00:29:40.670
and the RITM summer research
fellowship supported 10 weeks

00:29:40.670 --> 00:29:43.493
of field research for
me in Eastern Kentucky

00:29:43.493 --> 00:29:46.940
on mass incarceration
in Central Appalachia.

00:29:46.940 --> 00:29:48.916
Since the 1990s, Central
Appalachia has become

00:29:48.916 --> 00:29:52.110
one of the most concentrated
areas of rural prison growth

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:54.860
nationwide, and many of
these new prisons are built

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:58.590
on formerly strip mined,
mountaintop removal mined land.

00:29:58.590 --> 00:30:01.680
And so my initial research
goal was to document transfers

00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:04.821
of land ownership from coal
companies to prison sites.

00:30:04.821 --> 00:30:07.930
However, within my weeks of
first arriving in Kentucky

00:30:07.930 --> 00:30:11.833
this summer, one of my research
sites, there was major news

00:30:11.833 --> 00:30:14.640
that broke regarding
the pending construction

00:30:14.640 --> 00:30:18.060
of a new federal prison in
Letcher County, Kentucky,

00:30:18.060 --> 00:30:22.610
and this is the prison proposal
that was first proposed

00:30:22.610 --> 00:30:27.610
to the county in 2008, and on June 20th,

00:30:27.730 --> 00:30:29.910
the Federal Bureau of
Prisons withdrew its record

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:32.140
of decision to build USP
Letcher, which was projected

00:30:32.140 --> 00:30:36.544
to become a $510 million
new high-security prison.

00:30:36.544 --> 00:30:38.980
This effectively defeated
what was slated to become

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:42.560
the most expensive federal
prison ever built in US history.

00:30:42.560 --> 00:30:44.573
So due to this historic turn of events,

00:30:46.210 --> 00:30:47.947
my research took a different
direction, and I focused

00:30:47.947 --> 00:30:50.857
on the 15 years of local
and national organizing

00:30:50.857 --> 00:30:54.730
that made the defeat of
this prison possible,

00:30:54.730 --> 00:30:56.930
and you can see this is the proposed site,

00:30:56.930 --> 00:30:59.797
and this is a strip
mine, and I interviewed

00:30:59.797 --> 00:31:02.960
many different people including
some local landowners,

00:31:02.960 --> 00:31:05.280
and so here is one of my interviews.

00:31:06.708 --> 00:31:07.637
- [Mitch] And they were gonna try to split

00:31:07.637 --> 00:31:11.360
right across everything,
and they could bullied him!

00:31:11.360 --> 00:31:12.907
They coulda said, look, that's our coal.

00:31:12.907 --> 00:31:14.060
- [Sylvia] Yeah.

00:31:14.060 --> 00:31:15.040
- [Mitch] The best way to get to it

00:31:15.040 --> 00:31:16.740
is right across your land.

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:18.900
This has already been signed over to us,

00:31:18.900 --> 00:31:23.210
but I think from what I
done, they were forced to go

00:31:23.210 --> 00:31:25.141
another way, and again
they went up the road

00:31:25.141 --> 00:31:27.703
about half a mile and
(murmurs) winding road

00:31:27.703 --> 00:31:30.490
to the top of that, and all
of that coal was brought off

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:33.640
another way, but they wanted
to come off Brent Ball side,

00:31:33.640 --> 00:31:36.066
and had it not been for them
telling you got the right,

00:31:36.066 --> 00:31:38.220
you know, they done, they could ...

00:31:38.220 --> 00:31:40.568
And it woulda busted my,
right away my grandfather's

00:31:40.568 --> 00:31:44.330
big cow pastures where he
kept his cattle and stuff,

00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:46.580
he would've, you know, just
been off his mess, there it was,

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:48.230
he wouldn't have been
able to use it, you know,

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:50.520
it would've took up half of his land.

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:54.670
And it would've been terrible
to have seen it split down

00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:56.640
there, and that's what I
thought was gonna happen again.

00:31:56.640 --> 00:31:58.273
Really, really was.

00:31:59.120 --> 00:32:00.790
- That's what I thought
was gonna happen again.

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:03.550
That's Mitch Whitaker who owned 15 acres

00:32:03.550 --> 00:32:07.850
of this 700-acre site, located
his opposition to the prison

00:32:07.850 --> 00:32:10.413
within the genealogy of
organizing that his grandfather

00:32:10.413 --> 00:32:12.710
and his father were a part of,

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:16.150
was opposing radical
strip mining in the 1970s,

00:32:16.150 --> 00:32:18.090
so that's what you
heard him talking about.

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:23.090
And his refusal to sell
these 15 acres delayed

00:32:23.141 --> 00:32:25.490
the whole project two
years and was pivotal

00:32:25.490 --> 00:32:28.340
in ultimately defeating this
proposal, and he ended up

00:32:28.340 --> 00:32:31.950
joining a historic lawsuit
alongside 21 incarcerated people

00:32:31.950 --> 00:32:34.090
across the country that
sued the federal government

00:32:34.090 --> 00:32:36.090
against the prison being
constructed on the grounds

00:32:36.090 --> 00:32:38.740
that it would cause unnecessary harm.

00:32:38.740 --> 00:32:40.620
So this research is essential
to a journal article

00:32:40.620 --> 00:32:43.210
I'm currently co-authoring,
observing what this ROD,

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:45.660
record of decision withdraw reveals

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:47.925
about the current contradictions
of the US carceral state,

00:32:47.925 --> 00:32:50.903
and how a multi-sided coalition
of organizers, advocates,

00:32:50.903 --> 00:32:53.970
and landowners strategically
exploited these contradictions

00:32:53.970 --> 00:32:56.730
and created this space for
radical and groundbreaking

00:32:56.730 --> 00:32:59.820
opposition to the carceral
state to emerge in a place

00:32:59.820 --> 00:33:02.737
many might not have expected
or thought possible.

00:33:02.737 --> 00:33:05.070
(applause)

00:33:13.690 --> 00:33:15.870
- Hi everyone, my name is Aanchal Saraf,

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:19.690
and I'm a third-year in
American Studies and WGSS.

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:21.822
This summer, I did a
combination of ethnographic

00:33:21.822 --> 00:33:24.740
and archival work in order
to consider the history

00:33:24.740 --> 00:33:27.353
of indigenous anti-nuclear
organizing in the Pacific

00:33:27.353 --> 00:33:30.440
through an organization
known as a nuclear-free,

00:33:30.440 --> 00:33:32.320
and independent Pacific movement.

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:36.280
So the poster that is on,
this poster right here,

00:33:36.280 --> 00:33:39.172
as well as the two photographs above

00:33:39.172 --> 00:33:43.683
are both historical photos
from NFIP mobilization.

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:46.620
I also considered the material traces left

00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:48.903
on Pacific Islanders subject
to nuclear detonations

00:33:48.903 --> 00:33:52.400
whose landscapes and displacement
point to the still present

00:33:52.400 --> 00:33:55.670
nature of 20th century
wars in the Pacific.

00:33:55.670 --> 00:33:58.700
The aerial photo of Majuro
Atoll in the Marshall Islands

00:33:58.700 --> 00:34:01.290
depicts the ethnographic
space in which I began to ask

00:34:01.290 --> 00:34:03.060
the latter question more intently.

00:34:03.060 --> 00:34:05.883
And I took that photo
from the plane window.

00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:09.530
For my research, I visited
the National Archives

00:34:09.530 --> 00:34:12.127
of Australia and Canberra,
and the National Archives

00:34:12.127 --> 00:34:15.200
of Aotearoa, or New
Zealand, in Wellington,

00:34:15.200 --> 00:34:19.030
as well as the archives at
UH-Manoa in Honolulu, Hawaii.

00:34:19.030 --> 00:34:21.200
I also did preliminary
field work in Majuro

00:34:21.200 --> 00:34:22.870
in the Marshall Islands.

00:34:22.870 --> 00:34:25.770
I have a few primary takeaways
from this experience.

00:34:25.770 --> 00:34:28.740
First, the politics of the
archive are deeply enmeshed

00:34:28.740 --> 00:34:31.074
with empire's entanglements
within climate change,

00:34:31.074 --> 00:34:33.850
as many documents formerly
available in the Marshalls

00:34:33.850 --> 00:34:35.750
were lost due to disaster events,

00:34:35.750 --> 00:34:38.900
or moved to imperial archives
due to sea level rise.

00:34:38.900 --> 00:34:41.590
Second, the Black Pacific
is a generative terrain

00:34:41.590 --> 00:34:44.360
of contestation within 20th
century social movements

00:34:44.360 --> 00:34:47.110
in Oceania, especially
in the ways that troubles

00:34:47.110 --> 00:34:49.620
divides between Blackness and indigeneity

00:34:49.620 --> 00:34:51.930
in order to provide a
fulcrum for solidarity,

00:34:51.930 --> 00:34:54.650
and that's me, I think,
riffing on Manu Karuka's

00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:59.030
ideas of fulcrums for solidarity.

00:34:59.030 --> 00:35:01.640
Third, war is immediate in the Marshalls.

00:35:01.640 --> 00:35:03.330
It happened, it is happening,

00:35:03.330 --> 00:35:05.630
it could happen at any given moment.

00:35:05.630 --> 00:35:07.710
These coexisting temporalities remind me

00:35:07.710 --> 00:35:10.400
that as I imagine liberatory
Pacific Islander futures,

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:13.450
I must also account for the
colonial and settler futures

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:16.040
already set in the motion
by the artifacts of war,

00:35:16.040 --> 00:35:19.550
such as unexploded ordinances,
human bones in various stages

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:22.850
of repatriation or loss,
and American military ships

00:35:22.850 --> 00:35:25.510
resting in the fishing
lagoon, parts breaking off

00:35:25.510 --> 00:35:28.220
and breaching sand where children play.

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:30.478
Finally, Pacific Islander
women and queer people

00:35:30.478 --> 00:35:32.130
are and have been at the helm

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:34.250
of these trans-indigenous movements.

00:35:34.250 --> 00:35:36.620
The sovereignty of their
bodies is a critical foundation

00:35:36.620 --> 00:35:38.230
to further liberation.

00:35:38.230 --> 00:35:39.769
While these are just a
handful of the revelations

00:35:39.769 --> 00:35:41.680
I had doing research this summer,

00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:43.570
they speak to a much larger examination

00:35:43.570 --> 00:35:45.719
of nuclear colonialism
and indigenous resistance,

00:35:45.719 --> 00:35:48.580
as well as the transformations
of race that emerge

00:35:48.580 --> 00:35:52.090
from the migration catalyzed
by these unfolding processes.

00:35:52.090 --> 00:35:53.810
I thank RITM for funding this work.

00:35:53.810 --> 00:35:56.500
It has proven to be an
extraordinarily enriching summer.

00:35:56.500 --> 00:35:57.405
Thank you.

00:35:57.405 --> 00:35:59.655
(applause)

00:36:08.720 --> 00:36:11.000
- Hi everyone, my name's Monique.

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:14.800
I'm a Ph.D candidate in
the Department of History.

00:36:14.800 --> 00:36:17.750
So, two photo albums sit side by side

00:36:17.750 --> 00:36:19.750
in the archives at UC Berkeley.

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:21.819
One of them, filled
with mugshots replicated

00:36:21.819 --> 00:36:24.062
from a long lost set
of original photographs

00:36:24.062 --> 00:36:26.450
in the early 20th century.

00:36:26.450 --> 00:36:28.608
Under each mugshot, detailed descriptions

00:36:28.608 --> 00:36:31.340
of what the men wore, their hairstyles,

00:36:31.340 --> 00:36:33.490
and other information
that according to the eyes

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:36.176
of a Mexican anthropologist
and his research assistant

00:36:36.176 --> 00:36:40.830
told us just what "type"
of Mexican men these were.

00:36:40.830 --> 00:36:44.057
Another, filled with
photographs just like this one,

00:36:44.057 --> 00:36:48.000
of men standing in a field with
either cows, cars, or horses

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:50.169
in the background and little
to no information about them

00:36:50.169 --> 00:36:52.800
beyond that they're from the late 1920s

00:36:52.800 --> 00:36:55.350
and perhaps a name and a hometown.

00:36:55.350 --> 00:36:57.602
In another archive on the
other side of the Bay Area

00:36:57.602 --> 00:37:00.050
sit a different set of records.

00:37:00.050 --> 00:37:02.470
Mexicans entering the
US through Angel Island

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:05.900
whose names, birthdays,
kinship networks, jobs

00:37:05.900 --> 00:37:08.260
enter the historical record
through immigration workers

00:37:08.260 --> 00:37:09.988
and are found actually
in national archives

00:37:09.988 --> 00:37:12.530
of Chinese exclusion.

00:37:12.530 --> 00:37:14.664
All of these archives are
part of how Mexican migrants,

00:37:14.664 --> 00:37:17.537
from the middle class escaping
the Mexican Revolution,

00:37:17.537 --> 00:37:20.620
to migrant peasant workers laboring during

00:37:20.620 --> 00:37:23.260
the Great Depression in
agricultural fields in the US,

00:37:23.260 --> 00:37:26.400
enter the historical record,
shepherded in by how a set

00:37:26.400 --> 00:37:29.950
of experts or so-called
experts understood them.

00:37:29.950 --> 00:37:34.034
Anthropologists, economists,
INS workers, the border patrol,

00:37:34.034 --> 00:37:37.570
photographers, and bureaucrats
of all sorts on both sides

00:37:37.570 --> 00:37:40.840
of the US-Mexico border,
from 1910 to mid-century

00:37:40.840 --> 00:37:44.253
were heavily concerned with
Mexicans entering the US.

00:37:45.190 --> 00:37:48.160
But also of what type of
Mexicans were entering,

00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:50.701
of how to racially categorize
them, of how to understand

00:37:50.701 --> 00:37:54.920
and read what they are,
according to so-called experts,

00:37:54.920 --> 00:37:59.320
and what they saw in their
faces, clothing, and hairstyles.

00:37:59.320 --> 00:38:01.320
This is a story made up
of different threads,

00:38:01.320 --> 00:38:04.080
of how different experts were
establishing and solidifying

00:38:04.080 --> 00:38:06.770
the racial category of Mexican in the US

00:38:06.770 --> 00:38:09.570
at the same time that Mexican
experts were solidifying

00:38:09.570 --> 00:38:13.230
a national, almost mythical,
Mestizo-Mexican citizen

00:38:13.230 --> 00:38:15.220
after the Mexican Revolution.

00:38:15.220 --> 00:38:17.930
Both were taking place in
relation to massive migrations

00:38:17.930 --> 00:38:21.216
of Mexicans within Mexico and in the US,

00:38:21.216 --> 00:38:25.330
and they specifically were
concerned with how one saw them.

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:27.120
What did their clothes tell these experts

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:28.780
about economic station?

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:30.656
What did their hairstyles
tell them about their racial

00:38:30.656 --> 00:38:33.510
locations, about their
gender presentation,

00:38:33.510 --> 00:38:34.870
and about who they were?

00:38:34.870 --> 00:38:38.660
In short, what did one see
when looking at a Mexican?

00:38:38.660 --> 00:38:40.370
These are questions that enter the archive

00:38:40.370 --> 00:38:42.340
along with the photographs
and textual records

00:38:42.340 --> 00:38:45.100
that these experts have
used to try to figure out

00:38:45.100 --> 00:38:48.094
who these Mexicans were, how
they fit into the racial matrix

00:38:48.094 --> 00:38:51.074
of both the US and Mexico,
and how citizenship

00:38:51.074 --> 00:38:53.580
was being fashioned on
each side of the border,

00:38:53.580 --> 00:38:56.220
but also between them,
dependent on what one saw

00:38:56.220 --> 00:38:58.160
when looking at a Mexican.

00:38:58.160 --> 00:39:00.494
This is the project that I have
taken on for my dissertation

00:39:00.494 --> 00:39:03.120
and that has slowly come
together in no small part

00:39:03.120 --> 00:39:05.760
thanks to the generous
funding and support of RITM

00:39:05.760 --> 00:39:08.710
over the last summer, allowing
me to visit these archives,

00:39:08.710 --> 00:39:10.300
sit with the stories of these migrants,

00:39:10.300 --> 00:39:12.540
and to begin to figure
out what it is that I see.

00:39:12.540 --> 00:39:13.373
Thank you.

00:39:13.373 --> 00:39:15.541
(applause)

00:39:24.459 --> 00:39:26.920
- All right, I'm Adam
Waters, I'm a second-year

00:39:26.920 --> 00:39:29.160
History Ph.D. student, and first of all,

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:31.420
it's just really wonderful
to be able to share my work

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:33.933
alongside all of you, so thanks for that.

00:39:33.933 --> 00:39:37.074
So on March 24th, 1982, a rally was held

00:39:37.074 --> 00:39:40.046
at South Side Presbyterian
Church in Tucson, Arizona

00:39:40.046 --> 00:39:43.260
to declare publicly that the
church would provide shelter

00:39:43.260 --> 00:39:46.521
to migrants fleeing systemic
violence in Central America.

00:39:46.521 --> 00:39:48.576
Over the next several
years, hundreds of churches,

00:39:48.576 --> 00:39:51.490
synagogues, mosques, and other
religious communities joined

00:39:51.490 --> 00:39:54.342
the Sanctuary Movement in
supporting migrants and protesting

00:39:54.342 --> 00:39:57.990
the Reagan administration's
policy towards Central America.

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:00.387
Likewise, a constellation of
committees and organizations

00:40:00.387 --> 00:40:02.350
were birthed to shepherd the movement

00:40:02.350 --> 00:40:04.770
at the local, regional,
and national levels.

00:40:04.770 --> 00:40:06.590
And as is common with political movements,

00:40:06.590 --> 00:40:09.650
especially those on the left,
numerous and often serious

00:40:09.650 --> 00:40:11.643
disagreements emerged
over, among other things,

00:40:11.643 --> 00:40:14.619
the political orientation
of Sanctuary, and the role

00:40:14.619 --> 00:40:18.036
of Central American migrants
themselves in the movement.

00:40:18.036 --> 00:40:20.490
So the Sanctuary Movement was
the subject of intense media

00:40:20.490 --> 00:40:23.940
coverage and a good deal of
scholarly research in its time,

00:40:23.940 --> 00:40:26.285
but given where we find
ourselves today with renewed

00:40:26.285 --> 00:40:29.460
conversations about migration
from Central America,

00:40:29.460 --> 00:40:31.886
about migrant detention
and deportation regimes,

00:40:31.886 --> 00:40:34.980
and with the emergence of
a new Sanctuary Movement,

00:40:34.980 --> 00:40:36.720
I think it's an opportune
moment to revisit

00:40:36.720 --> 00:40:38.403
the 1980s Sanctuary Movement.

00:40:39.380 --> 00:40:41.450
So this summer I spent a month in Tucson

00:40:41.450 --> 00:40:43.407
conducting initial research
on the Sanctuary Movement

00:40:43.407 --> 00:40:45.560
and its historical legacies.

00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:48.220
My research was primarily
archival, exploring several

00:40:48.220 --> 00:40:51.127
Sanctuary-related collections
at the University of Arizona,

00:40:51.127 --> 00:40:53.093
but I also had the
opportunity to get involved

00:40:53.093 --> 00:40:55.420
with local organizations currently working

00:40:55.420 --> 00:40:58.170
in humanitarian assistance
and advocacy for migrants,

00:40:58.170 --> 00:40:59.995
many of which were created and staffed,

00:40:59.995 --> 00:41:03.400
sustained by former Sanctuary workers.

00:41:03.400 --> 00:41:05.130
My time in the archives
and my conversations

00:41:05.130 --> 00:41:07.470
with migrant justice activists
have sparked a number

00:41:07.470 --> 00:41:10.040
of questions that I hope
to consider moving forward,

00:41:10.040 --> 00:41:12.073
and so I'll outline just a
few conclusions from my summer

00:41:12.073 --> 00:41:15.110
and avenues for further research.

00:41:15.110 --> 00:41:17.728
So first, most works on
Sanctuary have either focused

00:41:17.728 --> 00:41:21.010
on a local level and its
manifestations in a particular city

00:41:21.010 --> 00:41:24.890
in Tucson, in Los
Angeles, in San Francisco,

00:41:24.890 --> 00:41:27.420
or on the actions of
its national leadership.

00:41:27.420 --> 00:41:29.110
But the documents that I found made clear

00:41:29.110 --> 00:41:31.090
that the Sanctuary Movement
needs to be approached

00:41:31.090 --> 00:41:33.340
as a multi-scaler phenomemon.

00:41:33.340 --> 00:41:36.140
I hope to document the
relationships and the dynamics

00:41:36.140 --> 00:41:38.420
between actors at various
scales in order to present

00:41:38.420 --> 00:41:40.220
a more comprehensive
sense of how the movement

00:41:40.220 --> 00:41:41.333
operated as a whole.

00:41:42.250 --> 00:41:43.840
My research this summer also made clear

00:41:43.840 --> 00:41:45.569
that the Sanctuary Movement
was a transnational

00:41:45.569 --> 00:41:47.750
and international movement.

00:41:47.750 --> 00:41:50.030
So Tucson-based activists
were working closely

00:41:50.030 --> 00:41:53.192
with church leaders in
Central America and in Mexico

00:41:53.192 --> 00:41:56.240
to develop what they called
an underground railroad

00:41:56.240 --> 00:41:58.760
that brought migrants through
Mexico across the US-Mexico

00:41:58.760 --> 00:42:02.190
border and into sanctuary
in the United States.

00:42:02.190 --> 00:42:03.900
So far no one has really
been able to document

00:42:03.900 --> 00:42:07.380
how this trail actually
functioned, particularly how

00:42:07.380 --> 00:42:10.330
it functioned outside of the
borders of the United States,

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:12.760
and so I imagine that my work
in the future will involve

00:42:12.760 --> 00:42:15.733
conducting field work in
northern Guatemala and in Mexico.

00:42:15.733 --> 00:42:18.030
And finally I think there's a
lot more to be done to think

00:42:18.030 --> 00:42:20.620
about how the Sanctuary Movement
fits into larger histories

00:42:20.620 --> 00:42:23.400
of immigration policy,
migrant justice activism,

00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:25.430
left politics, and progressive religion

00:42:25.430 --> 00:42:28.300
in the late 20th century
in the United States

00:42:28.300 --> 00:42:30.560
and in Latin America.

00:42:30.560 --> 00:42:32.770
I'm particularly interested
in situating sanctuary

00:42:32.770 --> 00:42:35.480
as part of an emerging
migrant justice praxis,

00:42:35.480 --> 00:42:38.560
one that stretches from the
1970s through to the present.

00:42:38.560 --> 00:42:39.393
Thanks.

00:42:39.393 --> 00:42:41.521
(applause)

00:42:45.725 --> 00:42:48.142
(low chimes)

